KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ noexcept\(true\) member: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ noexcept\(false\) member: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
Assertion 2 failed: W<int>::f's address converted to `void (*)()
noexcept' as well as to `void (*)()', and overload resolution chose the
first.  The cause was not the deferred noexcept-specifier (W<int>::f was
correctly not noexcept) but the function pointer conversion: noexcept is a
comment of the function type, which type equality ignores.  See
cpp17_overloads_on_noexcept_function_pointer_parameters.
